<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s1">
  <p class="st-k rv">01 · Challenge</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s1">Courses don’t know who is taking them</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    SkillCoach AI teaches frontend, backend, databases, DevOps, data, AI/ML, testing and project
    management. Its premise is that a learner should feel they have a personal mentor, with the
    reach and price of an app. The product had to replace three things a mentor normally gives.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Need 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">A path that fits</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Start from what the learner already knows, the goal they have and the time they can give.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Need 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Learning by doing</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Theory, practice and assessment together, with real code instead of only watching videos.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Need 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Help when stuck</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">A mentor to ask at any moment, by text or by voice, that remembers your progress.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      How might we make an AI coach feel like a personal mentor, one that understands each learner
      before it starts teaching?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s2">
  <p class="st-k rv">02 · Research and discovery</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s2">Where existing platforms fall short</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    I worked from the business requirements document and a competitor analysis that compared
    SkillCoach with ten learning products: course libraries, enterprise learning platforms,
    coaching services, AI tutors and bootcamps.
  </p>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Six areas compared</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li><strong>AI and personalisation</strong> AI was a course generator, an assistant or a general tutor, rarely a mentor</li>
    <li><strong>Microlearning and mobile</strong> Most had an app, but several were video-heavy or built around live sessions</li>
    <li><strong>Coding practice</strong> Several offered no hands-on coding or projects at all</li>
    <li><strong>Skill mapping</strong> Strong in enterprise tools, mostly absent for individual learners</li>
    <li><strong>UX and gamification</strong> Mostly corporate or structured, with little gamification</li>
    <li><strong>Certification and jobs</strong> Ranged from built-in job portals to nothing</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Each product was strong in one or two areas. None combined a conversational mentor, built-in
    coding practice and an engaging mobile experience, and that gap is where SkillCoach sits.
  </p>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Three principles that came out of it</h3>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Conversation first</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">The mentor is the main interface, not a chat bubble tucked into a corner.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Personal from minute one</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Ask before teaching, and show the learner why their path looks the way it does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Principle 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Practice is never far</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Every lesson leads to practice, a quiz or the code editor in one tap.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s3">
  <p class="st-k rv">03 · Defining the experience</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s3">From a stranger to a learning plan</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The main audience is people moving into tech from other careers and early-career developers who
    want to upskill. The app is organised around five tabs (Home, Chat, Courses, Code Editor and
    Profile) and eight flows.
  </p>
  <div class="st-stats st-wide rv">
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">8</p>
      <p>flows, from onboarding to the code editor</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">44</p>
      <p>mobile screens designed</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">5</p>
      <p>tabs: Home, Chat, Courses, Code Editor, Profile</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Learner 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Career switchers</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Non-tech professionals who need to know where to start and how long it will take.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Learner 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Early-career developers</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">People who already code and want to skip what they know and grow in their role.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Later</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Teams and colleges</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Companies reskilling staff and colleges adding training, planned for a later phase.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">The core journey</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Onboarding: who you are, your goal, the career you want and your learning pace</li>
    <li>Suggested courses from the mentor, based on those answers</li>
    <li>A short assessment in chat that places you at a level</li>
    <li>A personal roadmap, week by week, that you accept or turn down</li>
    <li>Lessons, practice labs and quizzes, with the mentor and code editor a tap away</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s4">
  <p class="st-k rv">04 · Key UX decisions</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s4">Four decisions that make the mentor feel personal</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Each decision answers the question of how an AI can earn a learner’s trust before asking them
    to commit months to a course.
  </p>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision A</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Onboarding that feels like a first conversation</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Instead of a long form, the mascot asks one question per screen: who you are, your goal,
          the career you want and your preferred pace. It reacts to each answer (“Great!”, “Got
          it!”, “Okay!”) and finishes with “Personalizing your profile…”, so the learner sees their
          answers being put to use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/skillcoach-onboarding.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three onboarding screens: Hello Tomal! asking which describes you best with Student, Professional, Business Owner and Other cards; Got it! asking which career you intend to choose, with Frontend Development and DevOps and Cloud Engineering checked; and Personalizing your Profile with the robot holding an Analyzing screen."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>One question per screen, then the profile is personalised</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision B</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Assess before showing the modules</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          A course page explains what you will learn, but the curriculum unlocks only after a short
          assessment. The mentor runs it in the chat as multiple-choice questions with instant
          feedback, so it feels like a conversation rather than an exam, and uses the result to
          suggest the right starting module.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/skillcoach-assessment.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three screens: the HTML Fundamentals course page dimmed behind a dialog saying a short assessment is required to personalise the HTML module and lesson plan, with a Sure, Get Started! button; the AI Mentor introducing itself and asking the first question, What does HTML stand for?, with three answer options; and a celebrating robot after a correct answer, followed by the second question."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>A three-question assessment, run as a chat</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision C</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Turn time per day into a timeline</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          “How long will this take?” is a question learners always have. The mentor answers it in
          concrete terms (1 hour a day means about 3 months) and offers the other plans as quick
          replies. After the assessment it proposes a roadmap that the learner can accept with
          “I’ll Take This” or turn down with “Not for Me”, and the roadmap then sets out the course
          week by week.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/skillcoach-roadmap.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three screens: the mentor explaining how long the HTML course takes at different paces, with quick replies from 30 minutes for 6 months to 2 hours for 1.5 months; a proposed HTML learning roadmap with seven topics and Not for Me and I'll Take This buttons; and the Road Map screen announcing the course is ready, with weeks 1 to 5 from Introduction to HTML to Forms and Input."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Pace in plain terms, then a roadmap the learner agrees to</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision D</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Lesson, practice and quiz in every topic</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Each topic has three tabs: a short video lesson with key points, a practice lab with a
          task and requirements, and a quiz. The practice lab places “Need help? Ask AI Mentor” next
          to “Open Code Editor”, so getting stuck leads to the mentor rather than out of the app.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/skillcoach-lesson.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three lesson screens for Module 01, Introduction to Web Development: the Lesson tab with a 6-minute video and key points; the Practice tab with a Practice Lab task, a list of requirements and buttons to ask the AI Mentor or open the code editor; and the Quiz tab showing question 3 of 12 with four options and option b selected."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Lesson, Practice and Quiz, with the mentor beside the task</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

